00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:17.160
Just about once every three days, somewhere
in the world, a large commercial passenger

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.330
plane crashes.

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:24.460
Most of these are not hugely catastrophic
because most of these are fatality-free.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:31.580
Of the 1,070 recorded crashes in the 2010s
decade, only 112 proved fatal.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:36.569
Even of these, the vast majority of passengers
survive an average fatal crash.

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:42.149
In fact, half of all deaths from airline crashes
in the previous decade are from just twelve

00:00:42.149 --> 00:00:43.149
incidents.

00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:49.170
That’s to say, most fatal aircraft accidents
are not the kinds you see in movies—the

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.659
kinds where there are few or no survivors.

00:00:52.659 --> 00:00:58.769
Most are more minor incidents with just one
or two or a few more unlucky victims.

00:00:58.769 --> 00:01:01.940
The industry, though, does not view them that
way.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:06.610
The truth is that people fear airplanes an
irrational amount, so in order for air travel

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:12.150
to be as commonplace as it is, they require
a standard of safety far higher than any other

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.350
method of transport.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:18.510
Safety and profitability go hand in hand for
airlines.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:24.399
Despite this, though, accidents are effectively
an inevitability for any of the largest airlines.

00:01:24.399 --> 00:01:29.930
Statistically, some of these largest airlines
are also some of the safest, yet none are

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:31.850
100% safe.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:36.240
Therefore, with enough flying, an accident
will happen.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.679
All ten of the world’s largest airlines
have had fatal accidents in their histories

00:01:40.679 --> 00:01:45.710
and so, with this level of inevitability,
airlines must have a strictly defined plan

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:49.179
for what to do after one of their airplanes
crash.

00:01:49.179 --> 00:01:53.020
So, let’s say an accident happens.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:57.299
This will almost certainly have something
to do with landing or taking off.

00:01:57.299 --> 00:02:02.650
The overwhelming majority of accidents involve
the runway, however, if an accident is fatal,

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:06.929
it is much more likely that it has to do with
a loss of control inflight or a controlled

00:02:06.929 --> 00:02:08.679
crash into terrain.

00:02:08.679 --> 00:02:12.709
Runway accidents represent more than ninety
percent of accidents, but only account for

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:15.879
twenty-five percent of fatal accidents.

00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:20.540
The airline really never is the first responder
to an accident.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:25.640
That is typically firefighters and other emergency
personnel, but as soon as word of an accident

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.750
reaches them, the airline will begin to mobilize.

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:33.420
Almost every airline out there will have what’s
called a “go-team.”

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.420
This is a group of people, typically working
at the airline’s headquarters, who train

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:41.930
to be part of the crisis response function
on top of their ordinary jobs.

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:46.299
With larger airlines, this go-team can be
comprised of as many as hundreds of individuals

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:48.900
filling a huge variety of functions.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:53.049
Larger, long-haul airlines might also have
smaller teams comprised of people working

00:02:53.049 --> 00:02:57.069
at their outstations who can more quickly
get to crash sites while the main team travels

00:02:57.069 --> 00:02:58.629
from a headquarters.

00:02:58.629 --> 00:03:03.200
Now, each airline will have different procedures
and protocols, but there are lose standards

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:04.819
adopted by all.

00:03:04.819 --> 00:03:08.680
Typically within three to four hours of a
crash, an airline's go-team will have assembled

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.050
and taken off from the headquarter’s airport.

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:16.470
Usually, a standby flight crew and aircraft
would be used for this dedicated flight.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:19.220
With them, they’d bring a go-kit.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:23.050
Considering accidents can happen quite literally
anywhere, this would include all sorts of

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:27.829
things like satellite communications equipment,
food, water, cooking equipment, personal protective

00:03:27.829 --> 00:03:31.709
equipment, warm clothing, tents, generators,
tools, and more.

00:03:31.709 --> 00:03:36.829
The team’s personnel are typically split
up into dealing with three main areas: crisis

00:03:36.829 --> 00:03:39.799
support, humanitarian support, and communications.

00:03:39.799 --> 00:03:43.430
Each of these would likely be split up into
a forward team and rear team.

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:47.989
If the crash occurs in a remote location,
for example, the forward team, including the

00:03:47.989 --> 00:03:52.330
mos t essential members of each of the larger
teams, would likely go to the crash site and

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:55.439
set up a literal base camp using the equipment
in the go-kit.

00:03:55.439 --> 00:04:00.770
Meanwhile, the rear team might set up in the
nearest town with accommodations and facilities.

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:07.230
At the crash site, a triage area is set up
away and upwind from the aircraft wreckage.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:12.469
This might be done by some of the crisis-response
go-team members, or by emergency personnel.

00:04:12.469 --> 00:04:18.540
There, individuals would be split up into
three groups—deceased, injured, and uninjured.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:22.661
The deceased and injured would typically be
cared for by firefighters, medics, and other

00:04:22.661 --> 00:04:28.090
emergency personnel, whereas the airline go-team
would likely deal with the uninjured passengers.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:32.651
The rear go-team will have, meanwhile, set
up a survivor reception center—usually at

00:04:32.651 --> 00:04:36.350
a nearby hotel—and arranged transportation
to that site.

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:40.590
The largest group in the go-team is typically
the humanitarian assistance team which is

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:45.250
made up of volunteers among the airlines’
customer service staff—flight attendants,

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:46.890
airport agents, and others.

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:50.750
By grouping the passengers together in one
facility, the airline separates them from

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:55.660
media, and centralizes them for investigatory
interviews and support functions.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:59.840
The aircraft’s surviving crew will typically
be dealt with by a different team which would

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.540
bring them to a different facility than the
passengers—which is recommended for both

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:06.270
legal and public relations reasons.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:10.250
Another subset of the humanitarian assistance
team will set up a facility for friends and

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:12.920
family of the passengers near the accident
site.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:18.080
They might also set up a separate facility
at the origin or destination of the flight—where

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:20.480
large clusters of friends and family might
be.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:24.200
The individuals there would be provided with
updates as a group, and then would be sorted

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:28.240
so that friends and family of survivors would
be brought to an area to reunite, whereas

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.370
others might be broken the news that their
family member or friend has died.

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:36.600
The role of these family assistance centers
is amplified in the case of crashes where

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:41.070
there is a significant, long-term search and
rescue operation, as is often the case with

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:42.370
crashes into the ocean.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:47.120
In the case of the Malaysian Airlines Flight
370 disappearance, for example, the family

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:51.030
assistance centers were in operation for two
months.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:55.110
The passenger and family assistance centers
might need to house hundreds and thousands

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:59.650
of individuals, respectively, so the setup
and functions of these is likely defined prior

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:01.680
to any accident.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.240
Airlines will decide, for example, that they
need a general lounge, a faith area, a psychological

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:11.660
assistance area, a press area, a phone and
internet area, and more and more beforehand,

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:15.340
and then it’s just about deciding which
area goes where once a location for the facility

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:17.000
is identified.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:21.460
With this and all other response efforts,
actions are proceduralized so that each member

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:26.140
of the team knows what to do, even in a fast-moving,
chaotic scenario.

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:30.860
Away from the crash-site, airlines will set
up a crisis command center in their headquarters,

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:34.810
including executives from each aspect of the
airline’s operations in order to coordinate

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:35.970
their response.

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:40.380
In addition, a call center is often quickly
set up and staffed to answer questions from

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:43.260
family and friends of passengers and the media.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:47.920
This is just a small part of the overall communications
efforts, though, because more than anything

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:53.620
else, probably the best thing an airline can
do for themselves after an accident is communicate.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:59.810
That’s why the third primary aspect of a
go-team, beyond crisis and humanitarian response,

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:01.410
is communications.

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:06.220
After one of their aircraft crash, an airline
is concerned with business continuity—they’re

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:10.350
concerned with how they’ll emerge from the
other end of the incident—and so their communications

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:12.980
have to be strategic.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:17.570
Crisis communications is a subset of public
relations intended to minimize reputation

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:20.710
damage after an incident at a company.

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:25.630
Within this, airlines want to be sure that,
to the extent possible, they are able to control

00:07:25.630 --> 00:07:26.950
the narrative.

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:32.200
That means they need to communicate fast and
loudly to drown out any other noise.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:37.360
So, they put communications go-team members
on-site to be able to disseminate accurate

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:42.560
information as fast as possible to the media,
survivors, and family and friends.

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:47.110
There will also, however, be a team back at
the airline HQ that will have to make more

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:50.310
methodical, strategic communications decisions.

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:55.400
Now, at the end of the day, there ends up
being two main options for responding to an

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:59.150
incident: accepting blame or deferring blame.

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:03.180
Academic research into the matter shows that
taking responsibility early typically leads

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:07.180
to a better public response and actually tends
to decrease the size of settlements that end

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:12.890
up getting paid out, however, the risk one
runs is that, by accepting responsibility,

00:08:12.890 --> 00:08:15.770
this can open a company up to legal consequences.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:21.160
Therefore, despite overwhelming evidence that
it is not the prudent move, organizations

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:26.370
will often defer responsibility until it is
absolutely certain that it is indeed their

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:27.370
fault.

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:31.680
If it is absolutely sure that an accident
is not the airline’s fault, such as in the

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:36.610
case of aircraft manufacturing defects, deferring
blame can actually shift public sentiment

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:40.969
to position the airline as a victim themselves,
but the evidence for such cases typically

00:08:40.969 --> 00:08:46.189
only becomes conclusive after years of investigation,
so in the immediate aftermath of the crash

00:08:46.189 --> 00:08:49.009
it can seem to the public as evading responsibility.

00:08:49.009 --> 00:08:55.870
Therefore, deciding whether to take or shift
blame is quite the tough choice for airlines.

00:08:55.870 --> 00:09:01.720
One of the recent case studies in crisis communication
involves Southwest Airlines Flight 1308 in

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:02.720
2018.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:07.509
Shortly upon reaching cruise after its departure
from LaGuardia Airport, its left engine failed

00:09:07.509 --> 00:09:10.829
and fragments were flung into the aircraft
fuselage.

00:09:10.829 --> 00:09:15.639
With that, one window was broken and, due
to the rapid decompression and fast-moving

00:09:15.639 --> 00:09:19.779
debris, the passenger sitting next to that
window died from her injuries.

00:09:19.779 --> 00:09:24.560
The aircraft ended up landing safely and there
were only minor injuries among the other passengers,

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:27.779
but for Southwest this was a major crisis.

00:09:27.779 --> 00:09:34.000
It was the first aircraft accident fatality
in the US since 2009, and their first ever.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:39.149
Southwest essentially took responsibility
right away—giving each passenger $5,000

00:09:39.149 --> 00:09:41.959
in cash and $1,000 in flight credits.

00:09:41.959 --> 00:09:46.770
They also, very quickly, issued empathetic,
personable statements which, combined with

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:51.350
their prior positive reputation, were effective
in persuading the public that they were truly

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:52.430
sorry.

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:56.310
The company also paused all marketing for
a month to let the sensitive situation fade

00:09:56.310 --> 00:10:02.180
away in public discourse, so, all told, this
incident and its response cost them about

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:04.480
$100 million.

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:08.980
In the end, after the investigation was fully
completed, it was determined that it probably

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:10.990
wasn’t Southwest’s fault.

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:15.970
The accident was caused by a tiny, largely
invisible fatigue fracture on one of the engine

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:17.060
fan blades.

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:21.300
Southwest followed all maintenance and inspection
protocols for this aircraft, but for some

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:23.800
reason these didn’t catch the issue.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:26.879
Therefore, the protocols were updated after
the fact.

00:10:26.879 --> 00:10:32.139
Southwest went for the expensive yet effective
crisis communication method.

00:10:32.139 --> 00:10:36.610
If they had deferred blame, they might have
been able to lose less money, but if in the

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:41.329
end it was found that they should bear some
blame, this could result in significant reputational

00:10:41.329 --> 00:10:42.670
damage.

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:47.040
In the immediate response to a plane crash,
the incentives of the airline and victims

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:52.810
are often aligned because the public responds
positively to positive treatment of the victims.

00:10:52.810 --> 00:10:58.910
It is later on, though, where incentives increasingly
split—victims often look for compensation,

00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:01.600
airlines look to minimize their financial
responsibility.

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:06.449
By that time, the public is no longer interested
in a plane crash.

00:11:06.449 --> 00:11:10.670
Crashes are interesting when there’s a burning
pile of debris, not when a bunch of lawyers

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:12.220
are sitting in a courtroom.

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:17.610
Therefore, that’s exactly why airlines put
a huge, disproportionate amount of planning,

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:21.649
money, and effort into the phase of response
with the burning pile of debris.

00:11:21.649 --> 00:11:27.460
It’s the most effective use of those resources
and, at the end of the day, to airlines, airplane

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:32.300
crashes are a financial problem.

